<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Town Council</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Our Town Operates</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Paraphrase articles and texts for clearer understanding. Communication: Facilitate peer discussions on various topics.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can paraphrase articles about local government projects using simple language and relevant visuals (e.g., shows an infographic with funding details and project phases and says, "The city council allocated funds to renovate the sports complex."). Can facilitate discussions on civic duties by encouraging classmates to express their opinions, using gestures to prompt responses (e.g., uses an open-hand gesture to invite ideas by saying, "What do you think about participating in community projects?"). Can clarify complex information from civic articles by breaking down details and using key visuals for better understanding (e.g., shows a flowchart of the voting process and says, "The article means that citizens should vote to have a say in town matters.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a complex infographic illustrating "The Budget Allocation Process for Public Services in Our Town." Students are asked to briefly explain one section of the infographic to a partner. This activates prior knowledge of civic vocabulary (e.g., *public services, projects, decisions*) and introduces the need for simplification and clarity.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to paraphrase a complex sentence from the infographic. For example, "A significant portion of municipal government funds is collected through local taxation and subsequently allocated to infrastructure sustainability projects." The teacher paraphrases, "This means our town council gets money from taxes and uses it to keep things like roads and parks in good shape." The teacher explicitly highlights the use of the passive voice in the original sentence and its simplification in the paraphrase. Pronunciation of words like *public, civic, community* (/ɪ/ sound) and *share, serve* (/e/ sound followed by "r") is subtly integrated and practiced during modeling.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I paraphrased, did I change the main idea, or just make it simpler?" (Expected answer: Just simpler.)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why is it useful to paraphrase complex information about our town council's work?" (Expected answer: To help everyone understand better, to share information clearly.)</li></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5-7 minutes]</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Town Council":</b> The teacher presents a short article titled "Understanding Your Civic Duties: Key Decisions by Our Town Council." The article contains sections on recent decisions made by the *town council* regarding *public services* and citizen *participation*, using passive voice and reported speech.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, authentic-looking news article excerpt (e.g., from a local newspaper or municipal website) describing two or three recent town council decisions and their impact. The register is formal-informal, and the format is a brief informative text.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student can understand and interpret information about civic responsibilities and local government operations to facilitate communication and understanding for others.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the article individually.
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main purpose of this article about the town council?" (To inform citizens about their duties and town decisions.)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match complex phrases from the article (e.g., "The resolution was passed unanimously...") with simpler statements provided by the teacher (e.g., "...everyone agreed to the new rule.").</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[7-10 minutes]</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Mediation.</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice paraphrasing complex sentences, using passive voice (e.g., "Decisions are made based on community feedback."), reported speech (e.g., "The mayor said that new policies would be implemented soon."), and modals (e.g., "Citizens must vote to have a say in community decisions."). Key vocabulary such as *town council, community, voice, feedback, decisions, public services, citizens, participation, responsibilities, projects* will be reinforced. Pronunciation practice will focus on word stress in *decision, participation, responsibility* and short vowel sounds /ɪ/ in *civic, public, community* and /e/ in *share, serve*.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
            <ol>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: "Simplify the Statement."</b> Students receive a worksheet with 5-6 complex sentences extracted from the article (or similar civic texts) that describe local government actions. In pairs, they must rewrite each sentence in simpler language, maintaining the original meaning. Emphasis is placed on using target grammar (passive voice, reported speech, modals) where appropriate for clarity. Example: "The municipal government's strategic objectives are frequently deliberated upon by various stakeholder committees." -> "Committees often discuss the town's main goals."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice: "Role-Play: Community Clarifier."</b> Students work in small groups of three. One student acts as an "informed citizen" who has read a complex civic document. Another acts as a "neighbor" who needs information clarified. The third student acts as the "mediator." The "informed citizen" shares a complex fact (e.g., "The town council announced that future funds would be prioritized for sustainability initiatives"), then the "mediator" must paraphrase and clarify it for the "neighbor" in simpler terms. The mediator also practices asking for opinions on civic responsibilities (e.g., "What do you think are important duties for citizens?") and showing respect in public dialogue (e.g., "Could you tell us more about your ideas?"). Roles rotate.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Peer Check & Feedback:</b> After each round of role-play, groups provide feedback on the clarity of the mediation and the effective use of target language, especially the passive voice and reported speech, and vocabulary like *involvement, authorities, civil duties*.</li>
            </ol></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[15-18 minutes]</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Town Council."</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will work in their groups to develop and present a segment of their "Civic Responsibility Campaign." Each group prepares a short (2-3 minute) presentation for a mock "Community Meeting." The presentation must include:
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">An explanation of a key aspect of their campaign (e.g., a specific call to action, a project proposal, or a description of how citizens can get involved).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least one instance where complex information (e.g., funding source, decision-making process) is paraphrased for clearer understanding, potentially using a visual aid (e.g., a simple chart or infographic). Example: "The allocated budget for the clean-up initiative was approved by the town council; in simpler terms, funds were set aside for cleaning our community parks."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least one instance of facilitating discussion by inviting questions or opinions from the "audience" (classmates) using gestures and polite phrases. Example: "What do you think about participating in community projects? We need your voice."</li>
            </ul>
            Students must use the passive voice (e.g., "Decisions are made by the town council."), reported speech (e.g., "Our campaign recommends that citizens attend public meetings."), and modals (e.g., "Citizens should attend council meetings.") as part of their campaign message and mediation. They should also actively use vocabulary like *involvement, participation, elections, responsibilities, projects, sustainability*. The teacher circulates, offering support and noting effective mediation strategies and language use.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10-12 minutes]</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents their campaign segment as described in Stage 4. While one group presents, classmates use a simple peer assessment form to evaluate the presenters' mediation skills. The teacher uses an observation checklist to note individual and group performance.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Paraphrasing:</b> Was complex information simplified effectively for the audience?</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Effective Facilitation:</b> Did the student successfully encourage participation and opinions from classmates using appropriate gestures and language? (e.g., "What are your thoughts on this proposal?")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Language:</b> Appropriate and accurate use of passive voice, reported speech, and modals.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Relevant Vocabulary:</b> Inclusion of key civic-related vocabulary (e.g., *participation, involvement, responsibilities, voice, feedback*).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sociolinguistic Appropriacy:</b> Using formal and respectful language in civic discussions (e.g., "Thank you for sharing your thoughts," "Could you tell us more about your ideas?").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of key words and appropriate word stress.</li>
            </ul></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket:
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Name one new way you learned to make complex information about our town council easier to understand for others."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did you help your classmates express their opinions during our campaign presentations today?"</li></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5-7 minutes]</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Reflect on your role as a mediator today. What was one specific strategy you used to clarify information or facilitate discussion effectively, and why do you think it worked?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can you apply the mediation skills you practiced today in other aspects of your academic life or daily interactions within the community?"</li></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students develop their mediation skills? The emphasis on real-world civic scenarios and the use of visuals seemed to increase engagement. The role-play activity allowed for practical application.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson or similar mediation tasks? Some students struggled with consistently using formal language in their facilitation. More explicit practice with formal expressions for asking opinions and showing respect might be beneficial. Also, providing more diverse visual aids could further support paraphrasing.</li></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "How Our Town Operates" theme. The skills developed here, particularly civic engagement and effective communication, will serve as a foundation for the next thematic unit, which will explore "Environmental Sustainability in Our Community" and require students to mediate complex scientific information for public understanding.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[3-5 minutes]</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>